<p class="lt-chem-31563">After completing this section, you should be able to</p>

<ol>
    <li class="lt-chem-31563">compare the reactivity of a typical alkene with that of benzene.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-31563">Use the heat of hydrogenation data to show that benzene is more stable than might be expected for &ldquo;cyclohexatriene.&rdquo;</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-31563">state the length of the carbon-carbon bonds in benzene, and compare this length with those of bonds found in other hydrocarbons.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-31563">describe the geometry of the benzene molecule.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-31563">describe the structure of benzene in terms of resonance.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-31563">describe the structure of benzene in terms of molecular orbital theory.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-31563">draw a molecular orbital diagram for benzene.</li>
</ol>
</div></section>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/15:_Benzene_and_Aromaticity/15.02:_Structure_and_Stability_of_Benzene" class="mt-section"><span id="Key_Terms"></span><h5 class="box-legend lt-chem-31563" id="Key_Terms-31563"><span class="lt-icon-default">Key Terms</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-31563">Make certain that you can define, and use in context, the key term below.</p>

<ul>
